=TITLE CHANGES

=head1 Upcoming

=head1 0.0.85

=item C<lint> warns about a block named C<=Include>, C<=Set>, C<=Config> or C<=Alias>, which is a named block and not the directive (rule C<directive-named-block>), and about C<=Image>, which the specification writes as C<=picture> (rule C<image-named-block>); each rule can be turned off on its own

=item C<assembleIncludes> takes C<reportCycles>: an include whose path names a source already on the way in is then reported as C<cycle> even when it loses no C<=set>; a mask that finds the file it is written in still passes it by without a word

=head1 0.0.84

=item a C<=set> before C<=include> reaches a paragraph or code written without a marker that the include brings first, and an include failing after such a paragraph no longer reports the assignment lost

=item C<podlite test> runs a test under each configuration that reaches a paragraph or code written without a marker in it

=item a file is read in the format its name gives: C<.podlite> and C<.pod6> as Podlite, C<.md> and C<.markdown> as Markdown, any other extension or none in the default mode, where text outside blocks is ambient; the extension is compared without regard to case. This holds for an included file, for the document C<convert>, C<query> and C<podlite test> are given, and for C<lint>; text from the standard input stays Podlite. A C<.md> file brought by C<=include> was read as Podlite, and a C<.txt> file as Podlite paragraphs

=item a C<:mime-type> written on the C<=include> line after the address of the source, before a selection, decides how that file is read: C<text/podlite>, C<text/markdown>, and any other C<text/> type in the default mode; a type that is not text is an error of the new kind C<format>, and the include brings nothing. A configuration that does not read whole leaves the line unread. It was taken as part of the file name

=item an included Markdown file gets the C<=config> of the including file on its blocks

=item C<formatOfFile>, C<formatOfType> and C<writtenTree> are exported; C<ReadFormat> has the value C<default>. A source may carry C<format>; the fourth argument of the reader and of C<parse> in an include assembly may carry C<format> for that one reading

=item C<podlite convert> and C<podlite query> warn when an included file comes from outside the root: the directory of each input file, or the current directory for stdin. C<--root> sets one root for every input. A file is outside by its real path, with links followed. The warning names the directive and the path as written, gives a mask the number of its files that are outside, and is given only when blocks of such a file stand in the assembled document; C<query> gives it only for blocks it prints

=item C<podlite convert> prints the file and line of the directive in front of every include warning

=item a source may carry C<external>, the reason it is external; an include whose blocks from such sources stand in the assembled document is reported once as an C<external> warning, with those sources in C<sources>; a comment counts, as it stands in the document. C<externalMessage> gives the text of such a warning for the sources a host shows. The origin of a node lists the directives it came through in C<steps>. C<resolveIncludes> takes C<root>, and a C<SourceProvider> may give C<real>, the path of a file with links followed

=item a mask of C<sourcesFromFiles> is resolved like a path, its C<.> and C<..> steps taken, and stays in its directory: C<*.podlite> no longer names files in directories below, as on disk; C<**> goes down

=item C<sourcesFromFiles> is a root of its own: a path with or without a leading slash names the same text, so a host can give its files by plain relative paths; a source keeps the identity it had

=item the lint context takes C<sources>, C<context> and C<self>: the C<include-resolves> rule checks a text that is not read from disk against the sources a host gives

=item C<podlite/lib/refresh-tocs> is exported

=item C<readerFor> takes C<body>: a block the host says yes to has its raw body read as Podlite in its place, with the settings in effect where the block stands; the blocks of the body stand at their places in the text, and an include assembly given the same reader brings what an C<=include> written in the body names

=item a provider of include sources names the address schemes it resolves in C<schemes>, C<file> alone when it names none, and C<locate> is given the scheme as its last argument: C<=include doc:Name> and an operand C<in doc:Name> reach a provider that lists C<doc>. A path that is not a mask names one source. When a provider says in C<failed> why it cannot give one, the problem carries the address and that reason and the directive stays in the tree; two sources with different ids for such a path are refused the same way. A source that is not found, with no reason given, is reported as before. A document that includes itself is told from its id before it is read. C<SourceStore.located> takes the scheme as its last argument

=item C<podlite lint> reads an included Podlite file with the Markdown and table of contents plugins and with the settings in effect at the directive: a heading written in a Markdown section of an included file is found by its address; it was reported as not found. A warning a plugin prints while an included file is read stays off the console, and an included file that cannot be read costs one finding and leaves the others in place

=item C<readerFor> makes the reading an include assembly asks for out of a parser, so a host need not write it; its fourth argument hands the parser the lists it fills with recognition events and diagnostics

=item the assembly of includes is exported from C<podlite> as well as from C<podlite/assemble>. C<locate> may say why a mask could not be expanded, in C<failed>; with C<tolerant> a text that fails to parse is a source that cannot be had

=item a table of contents written in an included file lists what that file includes: it is built again over the file once its own includes are in, also when the table alone is brought in by a selector. It listed the file as it was before them

=item C<podlite/assemble> gives C<sourcesFromFiles>, sources over texts held in memory by path, and C<assembleAsync>, which assembles a document whose sources answer through promises: it asks for what is missing and assembles again until nothing is. C<createSourceStore> keeps the answers between assemblies. Sources may answer C<undefined> for what is not known yet; the include then stays in place with its C<=set> assignments and nothing is reported

=item the assembly of includes is given as C<podlite/assemble>, an entry with no module of Node in it: C<assembleIncludes> takes the included text from C<sources> the host gives, where C<locate> finds the sources a written path names and C<read> gives the text of one. C<resolveIncludes> reads the disk through it and is not changed. An include problem that loses C<=set> assignments names them in C<lost>

=item a file an C<=include> brings whole stays in a C<root> block in the assembled document, so its C<=config> and C<=alias> end with it: in HTML and Markdown they acted on the blocks after the include. A selection goes through the block and does not find it

=item C<podlite test> runs a test brought in by several files once for each configuration it is brought in under; it ran it once whatever the configuration

=item on the first block an C<=include> brings, a C<=set> of the included file wins over a C<=set> written before the include for the same key; the one before the include won

=item a C<=config> written before C<=include> reaches the blocks the include places, as it reaches the blocks written after it; an included file kept to its own C<=config>. The nearer file decides an option: the file of the block, then the files including it, nearest first. The included text is read with those settings, so a C<:allow> they give opens its blocks to markup codes. C<convert>, C<query> and C<test> follow the rule

=item the selector of an C<=include> still reads the file without the settings of the including file. A found block those settings read as something else is placed as its file reads on its own, with the warning C<include-reading-differs>

=item C<resolveIncludes> hands C<parse> the settings in effect at the directive as a third argument

=item a C<=set> written before C<=include> reaches the first block the include brings, in C<convert>, C<query> and C<podlite test>; the block after the include received it. When the include brings no block, the assignment goes on to the next block of the same list, and to the end of the list without one it is reported with the kind C<set-target>. When the include fails, it goes nowhere, and the message of the failure names it; an include that brings nothing because its file is already being included says so with the kind C<cycle>, and does so only when it loses an assignment. Both kinds are warnings, and C<isWarning> is exported. An include that fails inside the included content, before any block, stops the search the same way: the assignment goes nowhere and its message names it. C<podlite lint> reads the document without its includes, so a link to an address a C<=set> gives an included block is reported as not found; before, the address landed on the block after the include

=item C<podlite query> reads a file its selector names, relative to the working directory, as it reads a file an operand of C<in> names: C<podlite query 'file:x.podlite | para'> needs no input files, a mask is expanded on disk as C<=include> expands it, and the files and input given are not read, with a warning. A source that does not resolve, and an address no file holds, is reported once and exits with 1; it used to give 0 matches. A file given is no longer taken for a source that only shares its tail, and a C<doc:> source is looked for among the files given

=item an include by a selector places a found block once: a block inside another found block comes with it and is not placed again, so C<=include part.podlite | *> brings a heading inside a C<=pod> once, and one table of contents. With C<*> the include brings no C<=alias> written at the top of the file, and an C<A<>> code that relies on it is left as written; an include without a selector still brings the whole file

=item C<podlite query> selects from the tree C<convert> and C<podlite test> read: a Markdown section is read into blocks, a table from C<data:> into rows and cells, and tables of contents are made again after the includes. A block with no place of its own in the file is given in C<--to podlite> as its Markdown, a row of such a table as one line of cells; in C<--to json> a block of a Markdown section carries the place of the section and C<precision: section>. The root inside a section and a folded section are not counted as found. C<--to md> and C<--to html> hide what the document hides. A query loads the Markdown, image and table of contents plugins when it runs, not the diagram renderer. The rules are shared through the entry C<podlite/lib/query-blocks>

=item tables of contents are made again after the includes inside the block that holds them, and C<podlite test> finds a C<=toc> block

=item C<podlite query>, C<=include> and C<podlite test> read a C<file:> operand of C<in> relative to where the selector is written: the working directory, the file of the directive, the file of the test. An operand that does not resolve is reported: the query exits with 1, the include brings nothing in and C<podlite lint> reports it as an error, and the assertion does not hold

=item podlite test runs the tests written in Podlite files: each C<=test> is reported as passed, failed or skipped, with the assertion that did not hold, the document it read and where a found block is written. C<--against> examines a document with the tests, C<--format json> writes the report as one JSON document, and C<--allow-skipped> lets skipped tests alone pass the run. The exit code is 0 when every test passed, 1 when a test failed or was skipped, no test was found or an include lost tests, and 2 on wrong arguments or a failure of the implementation

=item podlite/test gives the runner to programs: C<runTests> returns the report as data, and C<collectTests>, C<planRuns>, C<inputsFor> and C<evaluateAssertion> are its parts

=item an include whose selector cannot be read, or whose scheme is not C<file:>, is now a warning in podlite convert, podlite query and the include-resolves rule of podlite lint; it used to be left in place without a word

=item podlite --version prints the version of the package

=item podlite convert keeps a table of contents hidden when it is rebuilt after the includes are in

=item podlite convert builds a table of contents of the document after its includes are in, so the table lists the blocks they bring

=item C<:lint-ignore> on a block no longer silences a finding at the first line of the block after it

=item podlite query selects through C<=include>: blocks an include brings in are found, their text and the file in the json output are those of the file they are written in, and a selection of C<include> no longer finds a directive that resolved. An include that is not there is reported with the file and line of the directive, the other matches are still written, and the exit code is 1; the report is not silenced by --quiet

=item podlite convert stops on an include whose address is not in the file, as it already did on a missing file, and on a source that is a directory. The address is found the way a link finds its target: by C<:id>, or by a heading under its name or the form an output gives it

=item when two blocks of an included file share the address, the first is taken and podlite convert warns; the last one used to be taken

=item podlite lint adds the include-resolves rule: an include whose source or address is not there is an error at the directive, a problem inside an included file is reported at the directive that brought it in, and an address that names two blocks is a warning. A selection that finds nothing is not reported. The rule runs on files read from disk only

=head1 0.0.83

=item the C<./lint> entry can be reached with C<import>. Its ESM build called C<require> in two places, and a module does not have that word, so the entry died on load. Reading a config written as code stays synchronous, so C<readConfig> and C<resolveConfig> keep their shape


=head1 0.0.81

=item podlite lint reports a link whose local file is not there, and a link with no address at all. The file check is a warning, since a document under construction points at files that do not exist yet; the empty link is an error. A target with a scheme other than file, a bare word in a Podlite document, a home-relative path and input read from a pipe are all left alone

=item podlite lint adds the unclosed-markup-code rule, which the specification requires of a parser: a markup code terminated by the end of its block rather than by its own bracket is reported, so the line does not go out carrying the opening letter. Closure is counted the way the specification defines it, by matching bracket count or the closing guillemet

=head1 0.0.80

=item podlite lint adds the markdown-in-pod rule: a heading written with hashes, a row of bars and dashes, and a pair of asterisks arrive as text inside a pod block, and the message names the Podlite construct to write instead. Blocks that keep their content as written, the separator of a real table, and markup shown inside a code span are left alone

=item podlite lint adds the abbreviated-attrs rule: an abbreviated block takes the rest of its line as data, so attributes written there arrive as text. Reported only where the line holds nothing but attributes and content follows below; a named block, an example inside a code block, and an attribute written with nothing inside are left alone

=item the command no longer raises the diagram renderer, and with it mermaid and React, before it has read its arguments: only a conversion renders, so the plugin registry is loaded there. A check or a query now starts on a runtime that forbids loading an ESM package through require

=item podlite lint adds the dead-definitions rule: an =alias that no A<> calls, and a =data whose key no data: reference names, are reported. A mistyped key at the reference shows up as the definition going unused, which is the shape the mistake takes

=item podlite lint delimited-block-balance takes the markers inside a block whose name carries both cases as content, not as structure: such a block keeps its body as written, and an example written in it no longer reads as an unclosed block. An all-uppercase name is still read as structure

=item podlite lint table-column-width no longer reports a row whose cells are held apart by spaces while only the separator line draws bars: such a cell may fill its column to the last place and the table still parses. A cell standing on the boundary, and a cell pressed against a bar the row itself draws, are still reported

=item the check is reachable as podlite/lint — runLint for a whole run, lintFile and lintSource for the findings as data, with the types that describe them. The path no longer spells the build layout, and the check stays out of the package entry

=item podlite query --to json names the file every block came from, so a query over several files can be traced back to its documents. Text handed in on stdin is named <stdin>; the other output formats are unchanged

=item podlite lint checks a long list of files on several threads, which cuts a 126-file run from 1.64 to 1.14 seconds. Short lists stay on one thread, and the report reads the same either way

=item podlite lint finds .podlitelintrc.json or .podlitelintrc.js by itself — the nearest one at or above the first checked file, json before js. --config still wins, and --enable and --disable name a rule for one run

=item =include takes a recursive mask: file:./dir/**/*.podlite reaches the nested directories too, skipping hidden ones. A single-level mask stays within its own directory

=item =include takes a directory mask: file:./dir/*.podlite reads every matching file of that directory in name order, applies the selector to each, and leaves nothing behind when the mask names no file. Files the mask does not name are never read

=item podlite convert takes --render-mode with production or draft, and reads the same choice from PODLITE_RENDER_MODE when the flag is absent. Production stays the default and masks covered content; the flag wins over the environment

=head1 0.0.79

=item podlite lint takes C«:lint-ignore» from the document: written on a block it silences the named rules there, written on C«=begin pod» it covers the file, and C«=config» spreads it over a scope. The attribute must name its rules; a mute that silenced nothing is reported under the lint-ignore rule, and the summary counts what was silenced

=item podlite lint applies the file given to --config: a rule set to off is not run, and any other setting replaces its severity. A config that cannot be read, or a setting outside off, error, warning and info, stops the run with exit code 2

=item podlite lint adds the table-column-width rule: a cell that reaches or crosses the column boundary set by the separator line is reported, because the table silently loses a column when it does

=item podlite lint reports a reshaped table under the table-shape rule, with file, line and a place in the summary; the padded-row message names the cause

=item podlite convert writes to stdout when the output is a dash, and reads the document from stdin after a dash or from a pipe with no files given; without a dash it still writes a file as before

=head1 0.0.78

=item declare the node floor: the command line needs 20.19 or newer, because loading it reaches an ESM-only dependency through require

=head1 0.0.77

=item podlite lint takes text from stdin, either after a dash or piped with no files given, and names it <stdin> in the report

=item podlite query and podlite lint hand over the whole output when it goes to a pipe; the command used to stop at 64 KB and still report success. A write that fails now gives a non-zero exit code

=item podlite lint delimited-block-balance takes =begin/=end lines inside a verbatim block as content and reports same-name nesting only there; nesting that the parser accepts is no longer an error

=head1 0.0.71

=item podlite lint link-target-resolves accepts a link to a heading by name — exact match first, then without regard to case; it used to know only author-written C<:id> and reported an error on a sound document

=head1 0.0.69

=item podlite lint attr-nested-angle also reports a value closed early by a C<< > >> written inside it, and no longer fires on running text or on the contents of a verbatim block

=item podlite lint attr-continuation-dropped catches a value written in any delimiter, not only angle brackets, and stays quiet inside blocks that keep their content verbatim

=item podlite lint adds the attr-value-dropped rule — reports an attribute whose value the parser could not read and dropped

=item podlite lint adds the link-target-resolves rule — an in-document anchor link must have a block carrying that id

=head1 0.0.64

=item podlite CLI bin uses a node shebang so C<npx podlite> and C<pnpm exec podlite> run without Bun

=item podlite convert resolves C<=include> file references and applies the trailing C<| selector> filter

=head1 0.0.63

=item podlite lint C<syntax-valid> flags directive-like lines that fail to parse and are recovered as text

=item podlite convert accepts C<--base> (and env C<PODLITE_BASE>) to prefix relative C<file:> image paths

=item podlite convert renders plugin blocks (pictures, diagrams, formulas) through the full plugin pipeline

=item podlite lint C<media-absolute-file> flags absolute local image paths that a published site cannot resolve

=head1 0.0.61

=item add C<exports> map, top-level C<types>, and explicit C<sideEffects> to package manifest

=item replace top-level C<require('../package.json')> with a build-injected C<version> module. The published ESM no longer carries a C<require> call, so browser bundlers (Vite/Rollup) can lazy-load the package without C<require is not defined> at runtime

=head1 0.0.58

=item C<< podlite lint >> accepts C<--strict>, C<--format text|json>, C<--config <path>>. C<runLint(files, options)> signature in C<@podlite/core>
=item C<< podlite lint >> adds C<syntax-valid> rule — catches parse exceptions, reports C<file:line:column: error: <msg> (syntax-valid)>. Text and JSON output. Missing files report C<io> error
=item C<< podlite lint >> exit codes: 0 clean, 1 violations found (or warnings under C<--strict>), 2 CLI/setup error (no files, unknown C<--format>)
=item C<< podlite lint >> file loader centralized — auto-detects C<.md> → markdown mode (via C<parseMd> from C<@podlite/markdown>), C<.podlite>/C<.pod6> → pod mode. Both produce the unified AST so rules apply uniformly
=item C<< podlite lint >> adds C<heading-hierarchy> rule (warning) — flags non-monotonic level jumps like C<head1> → C<head3>. Descending and equal levels allowed. Applies to both Markdown and Podlite headings via unified AST
=item C<< podlite lint >> adds C<id-unique> rule (error) — flags duplicate explicit C<:id<>> attributes in file scope. Auto-generated node ids ignored. Silent skip when the file declares no explicit ids
=item C<< podlite lint >> output split into C<src/lint/formatters/{text,json}.ts> modules. Text format emits a trailing summary C<N files checked, M errors, K warnings> and colours C<error>/C<warning> tokens with native ANSI when stdout is a TTY. JSON format unchanged (array of diagnostic objects)
=item C<< podlite lint >> adds C<attr-nested-angle> rule (error) — flags angle-delimited attributes whose value contains a nested C<E<lt>...E<gt>> (formatting code or raw angle) before the closing C<E<gt>>, which silently closes the attribute early under PEG tolerance. Suggests multi-angle / guillemet / parenthesis / bracket delimiters. Source-level scan via Pegmill C<grammar-lint.pegjs>; runs before AST parse so the rule is independent of parser tolerance
=item C<podlite().parse(text, opt)> dispatches to C<md2ast> from C<@podlite/markdown> when C<opt.mode === 'md'>. Default and C<opt.mode === 'pod'> use the pod grammar unchanged. Enables a single parse front-door for full-document Markdown input across consumers (C<@podlite/to-jsx>, C<@podlite/to-html>, C<@podlite/publisher>, C<@podlite/editor-react>)
=item C<< podlite lint >> adds C<delimited-block-balance> rule (error) — flags orphan C<=end>, mismatched C<=begin>/C<=end> pairs, C<=begin X> nested inside C<=begin X> (verbatim trap), and C<=begin> blocks left unclosed at end of file. Source-level scan via the same Pegmill grammar that hosts C<attr-nested-angle>; stack tracked through C<options._blockStack>, EOF check flushes remaining items
=item C<< podlite lint >> adds C<attr-continuation-dropped> rule (warning) — flags indented C<:attr<…>> lines following C<=begin X>/C<=for X> that get silently dropped by the parser (queryable only on the directive line itself). State machine clears on blank line, content line, or C<=end>. Suggests flattening attributes onto the directive line

=head1 0.0.55

=item add C<< podlite query <selector> <files...> >> subcommand. Extracts blocks matching a selector. C<--to> selects C<podlite> (default, raw source), C<md>, C<html>, C<json>. C<--fail-on-empty> sets exit 1 on empty result. Match count on stderr unless C<--quiet>. Reads stdin when piped or when C<-> is given as a file argument
=item C<parseSelector> accepts bare pattern-list without a leading pipe (e.g. C<head1, code[:lang<python>]>); behaviour unchanged when the selector contains a C<|>

=head1 0.0.54

=item add C<< podlite lint <files...> >> subcommand (stub; rules land in upcoming releases)

